Open-Meteo's forecast at a site, or its archive of past forecasts

Usage: hpr weather open-meteo [OPTIONS] --time <TIME>

Options:
      --json
          Print one JSON document on standard output instead of text; errors too

      --latitude <DEG>
          The site's latitude, degrees north (south is negative); not with --from, whose answer is for the place it was asked for

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use color. Auto colors what goes to a terminal and heeds NO_COLOR, FORCE_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE. JSON output never has color
          
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

      --longitude <DEG>
          The site's longitude, degrees east (west is negative); not with --from

      --time <TIME>
          The launch time in UTC, such as 2025-06-21T15:30Z; it must be within the answer's hours

      --historical
          Ask the archive of past forecasts, for a launch already gone

      --model <MODEL>
          The weather model, by Open-Meteo's name, such as `gfs_seamless`; its best match by default

      --from <FILE>
          Read the source's answer from this file, saved earlier, instead of fetching it

      --offline
          Answer from the cache only, never the network

  -o, --output <FILE>
          Write the profile to this file, as JSON

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Examples:
  Fetch the forecast for a launch at Black Rock and save its profile:
      hpr weather open-meteo --latitude 40.865 --longitude -119.063 --time 2026-09-26T16:00Z -o profile.json
  Ask the archive of past forecasts for a launch already flown:
      hpr weather open-meteo --latitude 40.865 --longitude -119.063 --time 2025-09-20T16:00Z --historical

Guide: https://hpr.fusionspace.co/cli.html#hpr-weather
